= Behavior — Simple =

'''Behavior''' is what an organism or system does in relation to internal and external conditions.

Examples include:

* moving;
* feeding;
* communicating;
* learning;
* fleeing;
* resting;
* cooperating;
* deliberately remaining still.

Scientists do not completely agree on one definition that works across biology, psychology, neuroscience, and other fields.<ref name="levitis">[https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2760923/ Levitis, Lidicker and Freund (2009), “Behavioural biologists don't agree on what constitutes behaviour.”]</ref>

So researchers should explain exactly what they count as behavior.

== Behavior is more than movement ==

A frightened animal may freeze.

That lack of movement can still be a coordinated behavioral response.

At the same time, not every change in an organism is behavior.

Growth, digestion, and development may be better described as physiological or developmental processes depending on the question.

== Internal and external causes ==

Behavior can depend on both the environment and the organism's internal state.

A food smell may produce different behavior depending on whether an animal is hungry.

The same threat can produce different responses depending on experience, stress, age, or social context.

Behavior is therefore not always a simple fixed reaction to a stimulus.

== Tinbergen's four questions ==

Niko Tinbergen proposed four complementary ways to explain behavior:<ref name="tinbergen">[https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1439-0310.1963.tb01161.x Tinbergen, N. (1963), “On aims and methods of Ethology.”]</ref>

'''Mechanism''' — What immediate processes cause the behavior?

'''Development''' — How does the behavior develop during life?

'''Function''' — How does the behavior affect survival or reproduction?

'''Evolution''' — How did the behavior evolve across generations?

For example, bird song can be studied through brain mechanisms, learning, mate attraction, and evolutionary history.

These are different questions, not competing answers.

== Innate and learned behavior ==

Some behaviors develop with relatively little learning.

Others change strongly through experience.

Most real behavior depends on both biology and environment.

Calling something “innate” does not mean the environment is irrelevant.

Calling something “learned” does not mean biology is irrelevant.

== Learning ==

[[Learning]] changes behavior or behavioral ability through experience.

Examples include:

* habituation;
* conditioning;
* practice;
* observational learning.

Not every behavioral change is learning.

Fatigue, drugs, injury, or temporary motivation can also change performance.

== Measuring behavior ==

Researchers can measure:

* '''frequency''' — how often something happens;
* '''duration''' — how long it lasts;
* '''latency''' — how long before it starts;
* '''sequence''' — which behaviors happen in what order.

An '''ethogram''' is a list of clearly defined behavioral categories.

Good categories describe what an observer can actually identify rather than vague interpretations.

== Observation can be biased ==

Two observers can classify the same event differently.

Researchers reduce this problem by:

* defining categories clearly;
* training observers;
* hiding experimental condition when possible;
* checking observer agreement.

Agreement between observers improves consistency but does not prove the interpretation is correct.

== Behavior and cognition ==

[[Cognition]] includes processes such as memory, representation, reasoning, and decision-making.

Behavior is often used as evidence about cognition.

But the same behavior can sometimes be produced by different mechanisms.

So:

<pre>
similar behavior
does not automatically mean
similar minds
</pre>

== Behavior and sensory processing ==

[[Sensory processing]] allows organisms to detect and transform information from light, sound, chemicals, pressure, temperature, and other inputs.

Behavioral responses can reveal what an organism detects.

But motivation and decision rules can change whether a detected stimulus produces an action.

== Social and collective behavior ==

Behavior can involve interaction among several organisms.

Examples include:

* courtship;
* cooperation;
* aggression;
* parental care;
* flocking;
* schooling.

A coordinated group pattern does not by itself mean that the group has one mind.

== Human behavior ==

Human behavior is influenced by biology, learning, culture, language, social rules, institutions, and personal goals.

A complete explanation may therefore require several levels at once.

== Computers and robots ==

Engineers also talk about the behavior of software, robots, and artificial agents.

This can be useful when actions are defined clearly.

A robot behaving like an animal does not prove that it has the same biological mechanisms or conscious experience.

